    <div class="text">
        <p>In general, a game of chess is a decision-making problem—the goal is simply to select the optimal move from a set of possible options.</p>

        <p>In 1950, <strong>Claude Shannon</strong> described a theoretical approach to playing a perfect game of chess:</p>

        <blockquote class="blockquote border-start ps-3">
            <p>
                "With chess, it is possible, in principle, to play a perfect game, or to construct a machine to do so, as follows:
                One examines at a given position all possible moves, then all moves for the opponent, and so on, until the end of the game (in each
                variation). The end must occur, according to the rules of the game, after a finite number of moves. Each of these
                variations ends in a win, a loss, or a draw. By working backwards from the end, one can determine whether there is a forced win,
                the position is a draw, or it is lost."
            </p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>This principle also applies to the <strong>ChessGame</strong> application. It can list all possible moves and has the potential to evolve its evaluation criteria in order to select the <strong>optimal move</strong>. The next steps involve developing:</p>
      
        <h3>1. Evaluation Algorithm</h3>
        <p>To assess the advantages and disadvantages of different moves, numerical values are assigned to the positions of pieces on the chessboard.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>In a <strong>basic implementation</strong>, the evaluation algorithm assigns values based solely on the material worth of the pieces.</li>
            <li>
                <strong>More advanced versions</strong> incorporate additional factors such as:
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Piece mobility</strong></li>
                    <li><strong>Control of key squares</strong></li>
                    <li><strong>Piece cooperation</strong></li>
                    <li><strong>King safety</strong></li>
                    <li><strong>Pawn structure</strong></li>
                    <li>And many more strategic elements.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ul>
       
        <h3>2. Minimax with Alpha-Beta Pruning</h3>
        <p>The <strong>Minimax algorithm</strong> is a recursive method that exhaustively explores the game tree to evaluate all possible moves. It simulates future moves and calculates their potential outcomes.</p>

        <ul>
            <li>Using the <strong>evaluation algorithm</strong>, Minimax determines the best move for the current player.</li>
            <li>
                To <strong>optimize the search process</strong>, the algorithm applies <strong>Alpha-Beta Pruning</strong>, which:
                <ul>
                    <li>Eliminates <strong>irrelevant branches</strong> in the game tree.</li>
                    <li><strong>Significantly reduces</strong> computational time while maintaining accuracy.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ul>


